{"id":"2026-11-21-GKOS","date":"2026-11-21","ticker":"GKOS","name":"Glaukos","sector":"Health Care","industry":"Medical Devices","price":237.31,"price_date":"2026-11-20","timeframe_days":105,"headline":"Glaukos raised its sales outlook twice, made its first operating profit, and the stock held its earnings jump through the market's two-day drop","side":"long","timeframe":"About 3 to 4 months, through the full-year report early in 2027","thesis":"Glaukos raised its 2026 sales outlook twice in three months, from $620M to $635M up to $775M to $785M, which would be about 54% growth on 2025. Two new drugs drive it: iDose TR for glaucoma sold $76M in the September quarter, and Epioxa for keratoconus jumped from about $11M to $53M in its first full quarter with a permanent billing code. The company also made its first operating profit (0.4% margin, against minus 12.3% a year ago). The market may be underrating the new 2027 sales guide of $1B, about 28% growth, because management said it set that number to cover downside cases, including a Medicare coverage limit on iDose, and to keep expectations from running ahead the way they did with iDose. The timing is good because the stock gapped up 34% on the October 29 report and has held that gain since, including on November 19 and 20, when the S&P 500 fell about 5.5% and Glaukos barely moved.","wrong_if":"A daily close under 205 would give back the whole October 29 earnings gap and break the setup. The idea is also wrong if Medicare contractors issue a final coverage rule that sharply limits iDose, or if the company cuts the $1B 2027 sales guide when it refines it.","business":"Glaukos makes eye-care devices and drugs. Its glaucoma business sells tiny iStent implants that drain fluid from the eye during surgery, and iDose TR, a small implant that releases glaucoma medicine inside the eye for months so patients need fewer eye drops. Its corneal health business sells Epioxa, the first FDA-approved treatment for keratoconus (a disease that thins and warps the cornea) that does not require scraping off the eye's surface layer. Doctors at surgery centers, hospitals and private practices buy the products, and the company sells mostly through its own sales force. It also has 14 drug and device programs in development, including treatments for retinal disease.","segments_note":"The company reports two franchises. Glaucoma brought in $152M in the September quarter, up 38% from a year ago, though that was slower than the 50% of the June quarter; management had warned that summer is a slow season for eye surgery. Corneal health more than doubled to $59M, up 154%, with Epioxa about $53M of it. Corneal health went from about a sixth of sales a year ago to more than a quarter now.","growth_note":"Sales grew 58% in the September quarter to $211M, and growth has sped up three quarters in a row, from 36% to 58%. Gross margin rose to 85.2% as the two new drugs became a bigger share of sales (management puts it near 88% on its adjusted basis). Operating margin turned positive at 0.4% for the first time in the table, up from minus 12.3% a year earlier, and adjusted earnings per share were $0.47 against a loss of $0.16. One caution: reported earnings were only $0.03 a share, and the December 2025 quarter had a large one-time loss that the brief does not explain.","commentary":[{"point":"Raised the 2026 sales outlook to $775M to $785M, up $90M at the midpoint from the July range of $680M to $700M, and gave a first, very early 2027 sales guide of $1B.","speaker":"Thomas Burns, Chairman and CEO","call":"Q3 2026"},{"point":"The $1B guide held up even in the downside cases they tested, including a Medicare coverage limit on iDose. They gave it early so investors would not get ahead of reality on Epioxa, as happened with iDose.","speaker":"Joseph Gilliam, President and COO","call":"Q3 2026"},{"point":"Epioxa finished the quarter at more than 200 treatment sites, with contracts signed to pass 300. Demand ran ahead of plan and strained the field teams. Insurers covering about 90% of commercial lives and 80% of Medicaid lives now have a path to pay.","speaker":"Joseph Gilliam, President and COO","call":"Q3 2026"},{"point":"iDose sales should reach the low $280M range in 2026, up from the earlier $275M to $280M, which would be more than double 2025.","speaker":"Joseph Gilliam, President and COO","call":"Q3 2026"},{"point":"Spending will rise 25% to 30% in the fourth quarter, putting 2026 operating expenses near $630M, up from the roughly $600M guided in July. That is higher spending, not an earnings upgrade, though sales are still growing faster than costs.","speaker":"Alex Thurman, CFO","call":"Q3 2026"},{"point":"The gross margin gain should last because iDose and Epioxa keep growing as a share of sales.","speaker":"Alex Thurman, CFO","call":"Q3 2026"},{"point":"Bigger awareness campaigns for keratoconus and glaucoma are planned for 2027, so the September results came before that spending.","speaker":"Joseph Gilliam, President and COO","call":"Q3 2026"},{"point":"In July, kept the 2026 gross margin target at 84% to 86% and warned that Epioxa sales could dip in the September quarter while the permanent billing code took over. The dip did not show up in the results.","speaker":"Alex Thurman, CFO, and Joseph Gilliam, President and COO","call":"Q2 2026"}],"technical_note":"The stock closed at 237.31, well above a rising 50-day average (190.95) and 200-day average (150.33). It jumped 34% on October 29 after the report, from 181 to 242, and has stayed between about 219 and 255 since. RSI has cooled from 87 after the jump to 61, so the stock worked off the overheated reading without giving back the gain. Over three months it beat the S&P 500 by 41 points and health care stocks by 26, and its strength against the S&P 500 hit a new high on November 20 while the market fell. Volume over the last 20 days is 43% above normal. The price is 24% above its 50-day average, so pullbacks can be sharp; the average daily range is about $11.","valuation_note":"There is no useful price-to-earnings ratio yet, because the company only just turned a profit: reported earnings were $0.03 a share last quarter and the trailing four quarters still add up to a loss on an adjusted basis. The brief has no share count, so we cannot work out price to sales either. The case rests on sales growth (about 54% guided for 2026 and 28% for 2027) and rising margins, not on the stock looking cheap. That makes it more sensitive to any growth scare.","levels":{"entry_lo":228,"entry_hi":242,"stop":205,"target":290,"note":"Buy between 228 and 242, the middle of the range the stock has held since the earnings jump. The stop at 205 sits under the October 29 earnings-day low of 207.41 and about one daily range below the November 5 low of 218.77; a close there would undo the whole jump. The target of 290 adds the width of the post-earnings range (about 36 points, 219 to 255) to its top. From the posted price that is about $53 of upside for $32 of risk, or 1.6 to 1."},"risks":["Five of the seven Medicare contractors proposed coverage limits on iDose in May. They have until May 2027 to finalize or drop the rule, and a strict final rule would hit the main growth driver.","Spending is rising: about $630M of operating costs in 2026 and a bigger awareness push in 2027, so profit could stay thin even if sales grow.","Glaucoma growth slowed from 50% to 38% in the September quarter. Management called it seasonal, but another slow quarter would test that.","International glaucoma faces new competitors and payment cuts in Germany and Switzerland, and the currency boost has faded.","With no earnings-based valuation and the stock up 146% in a year, any growth miss could cause a sharp drop.","Not known from the brief: the date of the next report, the share count, the 2027 cost plan and an updated gross margin range."],"catalysts":["Phase 2 data for the retina drug axitinib and for physostigmine, a drug for eyelid inflammation caused by mites, both expected by the end of 2026.","The full-year report early in 2027, when management plans to refine the $1B 2027 sales guide.","Any final Medicare coverage decision on iDose, due by May 2027 at the latest, with a 45-day wait before it takes effect.","Epioxa site growth past 300 centers and the keratoconus awareness campaign with Stephen Curry."],"facts":{"financials":{"updated":"2026-11-19","adj_eps_name":"Adjusted EPS","quarters":[{"q":"Dec '23","id":"FY2023Q4","end":"2023-12-31","rev":82.365,"gm":77.1,"om":-46.9,"nm":-44.7,"eps":-0.75,"adj_eps":-0.63,"rev_yoy":15.6,"eps_yoy":null},{"q":"Mar '24","id":"FY2024Q1","end":"2024-03-31","rev":85.622,"gm":76.3,"om":-45.6,"nm":-47.7,"eps":-0.82,"adj_eps":-0.7,"rev_yoy":15.9,"eps_yoy":null},{"q":"Jun '24","id":"FY2024Q2","end":"2024-06-30","rev":95.69,"gm":76.4,"om":-31.3,"nm":-52.8,"eps":-1.0,"adj_eps":-0.52,"rev_yoy":19.0,"eps_yoy":null},{"q":"Sep '24","id":"FY2024Q3","end":"2024-09-30","rev":96.67,"gm":76.6,"om":-25.5,"nm":-22.1,"eps":-0.39,"adj_eps":-0.28,"rev_yoy":23.9,"eps_yoy":null},{"q":"Dec '24","id":"FY2024Q4","end":"2024-12-31","rev":105.499,"gm":72.9,"om":-27.2,"nm":-31.8,"eps":-0.6,"adj_eps":-0.4,"rev_yoy":28.1,"eps_yoy":null},{"q":"Mar '25","id":"FY2025Q1","end":"2025-03-31","rev":106.664,"gm":77.2,"om":-19.4,"nm":-17.0,"eps":-0.32,"adj_eps":-0.22,"rev_yoy":24.6,"eps_yoy":null},{"q":"Jun '25","id":"FY2025Q2","end":"2025-06-30","rev":124.12,"gm":78.3,"om":-18.3,"nm":-15.8,"eps":-0.34,"adj_eps":-0.24,"rev_yoy":29.7,"eps_yoy":null},{"q":"Sep '25","id":"FY2025Q3","end":"2025-09-30","rev":133.537,"gm":78.4,"om":-12.3,"nm":-12.2,"eps":-0.28,"adj_eps":-0.16,"rev_yoy":38.1,"eps_yoy":null},{"q":"Dec '25","id":"FY2025Q4","end":"2025-12-31","rev":143.121,"gm":77.8,"om":-97.7,"nm":-93.4,"eps":-2.32,"adj_eps":-0.28,"rev_yoy":35.7,"eps_yoy":null},{"q":"Mar '26","id":"FY2026Q1","end":"2026-03-31","rev":150.571,"gm":77.9,"om":-13.2,"nm":-13.1,"eps":-0.34,"adj_eps":-0.18,"rev_yoy":41